course: world
lesson: 95
title: Money, shops and work in a town
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Before a loaf of bread reaches you, many people work on it: a farmer grows wheat, a miller grinds flour, a baker bakes, a driver delivers and a shopkeeper sells — and each of them is paid from the price you pay.
explain: A shop buys goods cheaply in large amounts and sells them for a higher price; the difference must pay for rent, electricity and the workers' wages, and what is left is the shop's profit.
explain: Prices change with supply and demand: when many people want something and there is little of it, the price goes up; when there is plenty and few want it, the price goes down.
explain: Some jobs make goods, like a baker or a carpenter; others give services, like a hairdresser, a bus driver or a teacher. Firefighters, teachers and street cleaners are often paid from taxes.
explain: Example: strawberries are cheap in summer when farms are full of them and expensive in winter, when few grow nearby and they must be brought from far away.
fact: Goods are things you can buy and hold; services are work that someone does for you.
fact: A wage or salary is the money a worker is paid for their work.
fact: Profit is the money a business has left after paying all its costs.
fact: Shops buy goods in large amounts from wholesalers, firms that sell in bulk to shops, and sell them at a higher price.
fact: When something is scarce, so there is little of it, and many people want it, its price usually rises.
fact: When there is plenty of something and few people want it, its price usually falls.
fact: A bank keeps money safe and may pay interest to people who save there.
fact: When you borrow money you usually have to pay back more than you borrowed; the extra is called interest.
fact: A budget is a plan for how to spend and save money.
fact: Taxes are money that people and businesses pay to the government to pay for things everyone shares, like schools and firefighters.
fact: Paying by card still spends real money from a bank account.
fact: Adverts are made to make people want to buy things.
exercise: Why does a loaf of bread cost more than the wheat in it? | Many people's work must be paid from its price | Bread is heavier than wheat | Shops always double every price | Wheat is free to grow
exercise: Which of these is a service? | A haircut | A loaf of bread | A wooden chair | A bag of apples
exercise: Which of these is a good? | A bicycle | A bus ride | A music lesson | A doctor's check-up
exercise: What is profit? | The money left after all costs are paid | All the money a shop takes in from customers | The price written on the label of a thing | The money a worker is paid at the end of a week
exercise: What is a wage? | Money a worker is paid for their work | Money a shop keeps as profit | Money paid to the bank when you borrow from it | Money given as a birthday present
exercise: Why does a shop sell goods for more than it paid for them? | To pay rent, wages and other costs and make a profit | Because the goods grow bigger in the shop | Because the law says prices must double | To make customers buy less
exercise: What usually happens to the price of something scarce that many people want? | It rises | It falls | It stays exactly the same | It becomes free
exercise: Why are strawberries usually cheaper in summer? | There are many of them nearby | People do not like them in summer | Summer strawberries are smaller | Shops are closed in winter
exercise: What does a bank pay to people who save money there? | Interest | Taxes | Wages | Pocket money
exercise: What is a budget? | A plan for spending and saving money | A kind of bank card you use for shopping | A tax that every family pays to the town | A type of old coin used a long time ago
exercise: When you pay by card, whose money is spent? | Real money from a bank account | Free money from the shop | Money printed by the card itself | Money that never has to be paid
exercise: Who usually pays the wages of firefighters and public teachers? | The government, from taxes | The children they help | The shops in the town | Nobody, they work for free
exercise: What is an advert designed to do? | Make people want to buy something | Give people a fair comparison of all shops | Make products cheaper | Tell people to save their money
exercise: Why do you pay back more than you borrowed from a bank? | You pay interest for using the money | The bank made a mistake when counting | Money grows heavier the longer you keep it | Borrowed coins wear out and must be replaced
test: A hot summer makes many people buy fans, and the shops run low. What will probably happen to the price of fans? | It will go up | It will go down | It will stay the same | Fans will become free
test: Which is true? | A shop's profit is smaller than all the money it takes in | A shop's profit is all the money customers pay | Paying by card costs nothing | Services are things you can hold
test: A café sells cakes for £3 each. Each cake costs £2 to make, including rent and wages. How much profit is made on 10 cakes? | £10 | £30 | £20 | £3
test: Maria gets £10 a week and wants a £40 game. She saves £5 each week. How many weeks until she can buy it? | 8 weeks | 4 weeks | 10 weeks | 2 weeks
test: A town has no street cleaners for a month. What does this show about their work? | It is a service that everyone depends on | It is a good that can be sold in shops like bread | It is not real work | It makes no difference to anyone
test: An advert says a toy will make you the happiest child in the world. What should a careful shopper think? | Adverts try to make you want things, so check for yourself | Adverts must always be true | The toy will surely make you happiest | Only expensive toys are advertised
test: A small farm sells eggs to a supermarket. Why might the supermarket pay less per egg than you pay at the till? | The shop must cover its costs and make a profit | The eggs get smaller once they are put on the shelf | Supermarkets pay more than customers | Farms always lose money on purpose
test: After a frost destroys most of the orange crop, what happens to the price of orange juice? | It usually rises because oranges are scarce | It falls because people want less juice | It stays the same because juice is in bottles | Juice becomes free because oranges are gone
test: Jack borrows £100 and must pay back £110. What is the £10? | Interest for borrowing | His wage | The shop's tax | A present from the bank
test: shop buys pens for 50p and sells em for 80p, why | To cover its costs and make a profit | Because the pens get better in the shop | To lose money on purpose | Because the law says prices must double
test: why're the toys everyone wants so expensive at Christmas | Many people want them and they are scarce, so the price rises | Shops always lower prices when demand is high | Toys cost more to make in winter | Christmas toys are made of gold
test: Mia says paying with a card isn't real money. true? | No, paying by card still spends real money from a bank account | Yes, card money is free | Yes, but only small amounts are free | No, cards only work for big payments
test: my brother reckons when u borrow money u pay back exactly the same. right? | No, you usually pay back more; the extra is interest | Yes, you always pay back just exactly what you borrowed | Yes, and the bank pays you extra | No, you pay back less than you borrowed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between goods and services? | Goods are things one can buy and hold; services are work done for one | Goods are work done for one; services are things one can hold | Goods and services are identical | Services are always free of charge
exam: Identify the designation of the money a business retains after paying all its costs. | Profit | Wage | Interest | Budget
exam: It is given that a thing is scarce and many people desire it. Determine the usual consequence for its price. | The price usually rises | The price usually falls | The price remains wholly unaltered | The price falls to nothing
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning payment by card. | It still spends real money from a bank account | It spends no money whatsoever | It spends only the bank's money | It creates new money for the buyer
